    <td class="igtext" colspan="2" bgcolor="#B7F2FD">Here it is a list of the 
      pulse EPR spectrometers built/modified or maintained by me</td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"><span class="igheader"><a name="xband2e"></a> 
      X-band2 </span><span class="igsmall">(ETH, 2001-up to now)</span></td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" valign="top" align="center" colspan="2"> 
      <p class="igsmall" align="left"><a href="spectrometers/2pfs.avi"><img src="spectrometers/2pecho.jpg" width="250" height="188" align="left" border="0"></a>This 
        is a recently renovated copy of the most famous ETH's pulse X-band spectrometer 
        known as X-band1 here. A lot of pulse experiments have been introduced 
        using it in 90th last century. See T. Waker PhD thesis for technical details. 
        Currently Xband2 is used to test <a href="#sys2">new measuring system</a> 
        and its component as well as to make experiments. As example the picture 
        shows two-pulse echo, off-resonance, measured with AP200 averager (Acqiris) 
        under <a href="http://www.esr.ethz.ch/specman/main.html">SpecMan</a> control. 
        Click on it to see a short <a href="spectrometers/2pfs.avi">movie</a> 
        about echo behavior vs magnetic field:). 3.10.03</p>
      <p class="igsmall" align="left"><a href="#tech">back</a></p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <hr>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" align="left" colspan="2"><a name="qbande"></a> <span class="igheader">Q-band 
      </span><span class="igsmall">(ETH, 1998-2002)</span><span class="igheader"> 
      </span></td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <p align="left"><img src="spectrometers/qbande99_0ss.jpg" width="247" height="162" alt="0" hspace="5" vspace="5"><img src="spectrometers/qbande99_1ss.jpg" width="223" height="162" vspace="5" hspace="5"></p>
      <p align="left"><span class="igsmall">I invested a lot of time first to 
        get this spectrometer working , then to teach users how to deal with it 
        and finally to keep the spectrometer alive. I still do some upgrade here 
        (ER036 FTNMR gaussmeter has been implemented recently, thanks to Stefan 
        Stoll) but not so much as before. The spectrometer has 100 W of mw power 
        at 35 GHz, good acquisition system (E580 , Bruker, thanks to Peter Hofer 
        who gave us opportunity to be first who implemented such a system on home-made 
        spectrometer) and it is equipped with state-of-art mw components. The 
        photos are taken in 2001. General layout and top view of the mw bridge. 
        The most components, except isolators :), made using fin-line technology. 
        Some details can be found <a href="../papers/mypapers/grom01_2.pdf">here</a> 
        . To see user guide with more detailed scheme click <a href="../man/qe580_0900.pdf"> 
        here</a>. 3.10.03</span></p>
      <p class="igsmall"><span class="igsmall"><a href="#tech">back</a></span></p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <hr>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <p><a name="wbandw"></a> <span class="igheader">W-band </span><span class="igsmall">(WIS, 
        1998)</span></p>
      <table border="0" cellspacing="20" cellpadding="0" align="left" width="409">
        <tr> 
          <td><img src="spectrometers/wbandw98_0s.jpg" width="257" height="170"></td>
          <td><img src="spectrometers/wbandw98_1s.jpg" width="103" height="170"></td>
        </tr>
      </table>
      <p class="igsmall">I really enjoy a time in the lab of Daniella Goldfarb, 
        including the time to assemble and run W-band spectrometer. The mw bridge 
        ( <a href="http://www.elva-1.com/products/equipment/epr.html">http://www.elva-1.com</a>) 
        and the probe-head are from V.Krymov. For further details look <a href="http://www.weizmann.ac.il/chemphys/EPR_group/wband.html">here</a> 
        or<a href="../papers/mypapers/grom99_1.pdf"> there</a>. 3.10.03</p>
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
      <p><a href="#tech" class="igsmall">back</a></p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <hr>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <p><a name="xbandw"></a><span class="igheader">X-band </span><span class="igsmall">(WIS, 
        1997)</span></p>
      <img src="spectrometers/xbandw97.jpg" width="246" height="172" border="0" align="left"> 
      <p><span class="igsmall">Here my job was to teach students how to do ENDOR 
        on X-band. And I did a lot of ENDOR experiments myself too! Some examples, 
        as well as spectrometer description you can find <a href="../papers/mypapers/grom98.pdf">here</a>. 
        3.10.03 </span></p>
      <p> <span class="igsmall"><a href="#tech">back</a></span></p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <hr>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <p class="igheader"><a name="qbandk"></a>Q-band <span class="igsmall">(KSU, 
        1990-1996)</span></p>
      <table border="0" cellspacing="20" cellpadding="0" align="left">
        <tr> 
          <td><img src="spectrometers/qbandk94ss.jpg" width="247" height="169"></td>
        </tr>
      </table>
      <p class="igsmall">My first spectrometer was a 35 GHz pulse EPR apparatus. 
        Homemade in full sense of this word:). It means that I'm not only assembled 
        the mw components which we buy or got for free from colleagues but also 
        it was necessary to fix some of these components and built all analog 
        control circuits of the pulse bridge as well as construct whole PC controlled 
        <a href="#sysksu">measuring system</a>. The spectrometer was made using 
        solid state mw components only. No klystrons, no tubes. It has three (!) 
        mw sources, two are service 50 mW cw Gunn oscillators and third one is 
        pulsed IMPATT oscillator based on two silicon double drift diodes. The 
        frequency of the first Gunn was voltage controlled (varactor) and this 
        source was used for a probe-head tuning. The second Gunn is one of most 
        stable free running mw sources (no DR, no external thermostabilization, 
        only a diode camera , stabilizing cavity and isolator) I ever seen. It 
        was used for cw (in absorption mode!) and saturation recovery measurements. 
        The key component is the pulsed IMPATT. It was designed by top level artist(s) 
        in microwave engineering. Two diodes ( p-p+-n+-n ) were fixed in coaxial 
        lines and then placed at certain positions in a section of WR28 waveguide. 
        By proper adjustment of the diode positions and an amplitude and a shape 
        of the current pulses applied to the diodes I was able to reach 2 W power 
        with the negligible frequency chirp. There are many others beautiful components 
        inside, some details are mentioned <a href="../papers/mypapers/grom92.pdf">there</a>. 
        Rafail (left bottom) and Yurii (probably making this photo) built even 
        a loop-gap resonator for 35 GHz.. 3.10.03</p>
      <p><a href="#tech" class="igsmall">back</a></p>
    </td>
  </tr>
