  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <p><a name="sys2"></a><span class="igheader">Measuring system on PCI </span><span class="igsmall">(ETH, 
        2003-)</span></p>
      <p><img src="electronics/sys2s.jpg" width="351" height="248" align="left"><span class="igsmall"> 
        This supposed to be a next generation of the acquisition systems for epr@ETH. 
        PCI bus is chosen as a system integration bus and recent PCI boards are 
        selected to fit the most demanding tasks in pulse EPR. One of such components 
        is in operation already. It is a 2 GS/s real time averager (3) from Acqiris. 
        It has dual-input with tunable gain and offset, 500 MHz bandwidth and 
        it's able to accumulate up to 64k traces at once. An acquisition software 
        is the Spectrometer Manager (<a href="http://www.esr.ethz.ch/specman/SpecMan.html">SpecMan</a>). 
        In a next step we are going to to implement 1 GS/s PCI boards (7) and 
        (8) which will provide two RF outputs and 12 pulse channels correspondingly. 
        Recent <a href="../posters/denver03.pdf">poster</a> (Denver,2003). 3.01.04.</span></p>
      <p><a href="#tech">back</a></p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <hr>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igheader" colspan="2"> 
      <p><a name="prog"></a> Pulse programmer:<span class="igheader">ASIC+DSP</span><span class="igsmall"> 
        (ETH, 1999-2003)</span></p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr> 
    <td class="igtext" colspan="2"> 
      <p class="igsmall"><img src="electronics/asictn.jpg" width="160" height="174" align="left" alt="5" border="0">We 
        put a lot of efforts to design and build ourselves a modern pulse programmer. 
        Jaap Shane, who was very enthusiastic about electronics, and Boris Glass 
        designed an ASIC for the pulse EPR applications (see picture on the left). 
        Then I was driving this project trying to get working the complete system: 
        PC-Texas DSP-ASICs. The team: Joerg Forrer and Rene Tschaggelar (electronic 
        engineers), Jurg Keller (programmer ). Finally I was able to record ESEEM 
        and HYSCORE in <em>real time</em> (monotonically at about 1.5 kHz from 
        the beginning of a experiment till its end) using this programmer. 3.10.03</p>
      <p class="igsmall"><a href="#tech">back</a></p>
    </td>
  </tr>
